Friction blister on the posterior heel from continuing to walk in ill-fitting footwear
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Tense posterior heel vesicle, Clear serous fluid, Direct correlation to footwear friction

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-008
Reason for Visit: Acute severe foot pain and impaired ambulation requiring assessment of a tense epidermal vesicle to rule out deep soft tissue infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe localized foot pain and a fluid-filled lesion impairing weight-bearing. Urgent assessment was required prior to the next in-hours period to rule out infected bursitis or diabetic foot complication and to provide safe decompression and barrier protection.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I wore new shoes all day and now I have a huge, painful water blister on my heel.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes an intensely painful burning sensation upon localized pressure on the posterior heel, progressively worsening over the past 12 hours. Reports a history of repetitive mechanical shear force from walking in new, ill-fitting footwear. Notes relief of pain upon removal of footwear. Describes a tense fluid-filled vesicle containing clear serous fluid. Denies any purulent drainage, foul odor, or systemic fever.
Functional Status: Antalgic gait, avoids bearing weight on the affected heel.
Social/Safety Concerns: Temporary impairment in mobility affecting daily activities.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/80 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable at rest, grimacing upon heel palpation.
Physical Exam: Foot examination reveals a tense fluid-filled vesicle located on the posterior calcaneal skin. The epidermal roof is intact without active bleeding. Diagnostic triad confirmed: tense posterior heel vesicle, clear serous fluid contained within, and direct correlation to footwear friction. A surrounding rim of blanching erythema is noted. Normal capillary refill in the surrounding digits. Absence of purulent drainage or fluctuant deep tissue involvement.
Environmental Observations: Problematic rigid leather shoes observed near the entryway.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No history of diabetes or peripheral neuropathy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Friction blister on the posterior heel from continuing to walk in ill-fitting footwear - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Retrocalcaneal Bursitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Superficial epidermal nature of the fluid collection and clear correlation to acute mechanical friction rather than deep inflammatory pain.

Bullous Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear serous fluid rather than turbid/purulent fluid, and isolated location strictly tied to a friction zone.

Diabetic Foot Ulcer/Neuropathic Blister - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact pain sensation, normal capillary refill, and absence of diabetes history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe pain and a large fluid-filled lesion on a major weight-bearing surface, significantly impairing mobility. Urgent evaluation was necessary to reliably exclude an infected deep space collection or compromised neurovascular status, particularly critical if underlying comorbidities were present. The face-to-face assessment ensured the lesion was an uninfected mechanical blister, allowing for safe, sterile management and immediate restoration of functional ambulation, which could not be safely delayed.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Sterile drainage of the tense blister via needle puncture at the margin to relieve pressure, leaving the epidermal roof intact as a biologic dressing. Application of a hydrocolloid blister dressing.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply specialized blister prevention tape to high-friction areas before wearing rigid shoes, ensuring the barrier takes the shear force rather than the skin.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Hydrocolloid dressings and preventative friction tape.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the area becomes increasingly red, hot, or painful.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if red streaks travel up the leg, if the foot becomes entirely swollen and hot, or if systemic fever develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a mechanical injury; secondary infection remains a risk if the epidermal roof is avulsed.
Clinical Course: Pain should immediately improve following decompression. The underlying skin will re-epithelialize over 5-7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the fluid inside the blister turns yellow or green, if foul odor develops, or if the redness expands rapidly.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Draining the fluid relieves the pressure causing the intense pain. Leaving the dead skin flap in place provides the best sterile, natural bandage for the raw skin underneath.
Actionable Steps: Do not peel off the dead skin. Keep the hydrocolloid dressing in place until it naturally peels off. Wear open-backed shoes until healed.

